QB.add(86,[{"n":1,"q":"A clinician notes relative bradycardia in a patient with fever and respiratory symptoms. Which of the following infections is most commonly associated with relative bradycardia?","o":[{"t":"Brucellosis"},{"t":"Influenza"},{"t":"Streptococcal Pharyngitis"},{"t":"Tuberculosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Brucellosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relative bradycardia is where a person's pulse rate is lower than expected for their body temperature."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brucellosis, caused by Brucella melitensis , is associated with relative bradycardia due to the release of inflammatory cytokines and increased vagal tone, even in the presence of high fever."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Causes of Relative Bradycardia in Infectious Diseases:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infection with Brucella melitensis leads to relative bradycardia due to inflammatory cytokines and increased vagal tone, despite high fever."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients may exhibit relative bradycardia associated with myocardial injury and systemic inflammation, showing insufficient heart rate increase despite high fever and hypoxemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caused by Salmonella typhi , this infection is known for relative bradycardia, often alongside sustained fever and systemic symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Linked to relative bradycardia, although the exact mechanisms are unclear."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can present with relative bradycardia as a clinical feature."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Noted in patients with Ebola, indicating its significance in severe viral infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Influenza (Option B) typically does not present with relative bradycardia; due to its systemic effects, it often leads to tachycardia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Streptococcal Pharyngitis (Option C) is not associated with relative bradycardia; patients generally exhibit normal heart rates or tachycardia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberculosis (Option D) can lead to various systemic symptoms but is not specifically linked to relative bradycardia."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 28-year-old male presents with a history of fever, malaise, and abdominal discomfort. His temperature has been rising stepwise over the past five days. Physical examination reveals bradycardia and a faint rash on the abdomen. Blood cultures are positive for Salmonella typhi. Based on the diagnosis, what is the most likely fever pattern?","o":[{"t":"Intermittent fever"},{"t":"Remittent fever"},{"t":"Pel-Ebstein fever"},{"t":"Sustained fever"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Sustained fever"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sustained fever is characterized by a continuous elevated temperature that does not fluctuate significantly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is commonly seen in typhoid fever, where the fever progressively rises in a stepwise manner over several days."},{"k":"img","v":10492},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Patterns of Fever:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fever Description Associated Conditions Fever Graph Pattern"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sustain Continuous elevated Typhoid fever, tuberculosis, ed Fev temperature. bacterial pneumonia, er neoplasms, connective"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tissue diseases"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Intermit Fever spikes followed by Malaria, brucellosis tent Fe normal temperature. ver (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":10497},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Remitte Fever fluctuates above Various infectious nt Feve normal without returning diseases r to baseline. (Option B"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hectic High temperatures with Severe infections like Fever wide fluctuations. bacteremia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pel-Ebs Weeks of high fevers Hodgkin's disease tein Fe followed by normal ver periods. (Option C ruled"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Double Two fever spikes within Adult Still's disease, Quotidian Fever\" Quotidi 24 hours. malaria data-author=\"Jack an Fev Edelbrock, 2018\" er data-hash=\"8497\""},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"data-license=\"CC BY-SA 4.0\" data-source=\"https://c ommons.wikimedia.org/wik i/File:Fever_Patterns_v1.2. svg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"99\" src=\"https://i mage.prepladder.com/note s/tDrtHXNo7S7w7ALipVT4 1744285290.png\" width=\"200\" />"},{"k":"img","v":10503},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tertian Fever occurs every third Malaria Fever day."},{"k":"img","v":10508},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Quarta Fever occurs every fourth Malaria n Fever day."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Periodi Regular intervals of fever Cyclic neutropenia c Fever episodes. Syndro mes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Specific Infections and Fever Patterns"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Infection Fever Pattern Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Malaria P. vivax / P. ovale : alternate days;"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"P. malariae : every third day; P. falciparum : no specific pattern"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dengue Continuous or \"saddle-back\" fever with breaks on days"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"4-5"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Yellow Fever Recrudescence after initial resolution with back pain,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"jaundice"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Louse-borne Relapsing Fever Crisis after 4-10 days, profuse sweating, possible"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"relapses"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Typhoid Fever Stepladder rise over 4-5 days with malaise and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"headaches"}]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following statements about malarial fever is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"Quotidian fever is associated with P. falciparum infection ."},{"t":"Tertian fever occurs every 48 hours."},{"t":"Quartan fever is linked to P. malariae."},{"t":"Quotidian fever is associated with P. ovale."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Quotidian fever is associated with P. ovale."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Quotidian fever refers to a fever pattern that occurs daily, returning to baseline each day and is typically associated with infections such as P. falciparum and P. knowlesi."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Malaria fever patterns"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type of Fever Characteristics Examples"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Quotidian Fever Fever occurs once every day, and it P. falciparum, P. knowlesi (Option A) will touch the baseline."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tertian Fever (Option B) Fever is occurring every 48 hours. P. vivax, P. ovale"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Quartan Fever (Option C) Fever will occur after every 72 hours. P. malariae"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 55-year-old male presents to the emergency department with a fever of 38.5°C (101.3°F) that has persisted for three weeks. He reports significant weight loss, night sweats, and fatigue. His medical history is unremarkable. On physical examination, he has splenomegaly and mild lymphadenopathy. Which investigation would be the most appropriate next step in this patient's management?","o":[{"t":"Chest X-ray"},{"t":"Blood culture"},{"t":"CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis"},{"t":"HIV test"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Given the patient's symptoms of prolonged fever, significant weight loss, night sweats, splenomegaly, and lymphadenopathy, a CT scan can help identify potential underlying causes such as infections, malignancies (e.g., lymphomas), or abscesses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It provides a comprehensive view of the abdominal organs and lymphatic structures."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PUO-causes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Category Common Causes Other Causes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infections Tuberculosis (military or Emerging Infections: HIV,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"disseminated / extra-pulmonary) Leishmaniasis, Melioidosis Endocarditis (culture negative) Travelers: Malaria, Visceral Deep-seated infections (abscesses, leishmaniasis, Histoplasmosis prostatitis, etc.) Typhoidal and non-typhoidal Salmonella"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neoplasms Malignant lymphomas Renal-cell carcinoma, hepatocellular"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cancer Ovarian cancer, atrial myxoma Castleman disease, brain tumors"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Auto-inflammatory and Giant-cell arteritis, Polymyalgia Inflammatory bowel disease, certain Autoimmune Disorders rheumatica (elderly) types of uveitis, Behçet's syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Adult-onset Still's disease (younger) Rheumatoid arthritis, systemic lupus"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"erythematosus, dermatomyositis, polymyositis PAN, small vessel vasculitis, FMF"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Miscellaneous Granulomatous: Kikuchi disease, Cirrhosis, Pancreatitis, Factitious"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Sarcoidosis, Granulomatous fever hepatitis Drug fever (3-7% of febrile episodes Vascular: atrial myxoma, pulmonary in hospitalized patients; 1/3 due to emboli, hematomas antibiotics - beta-lactams) Endocrine: thyrotoxicosis, DRESS, Hyperthermic drug thyroiditis, Pheochromocytoma syndromes (NMS, Serotonin Hematologic: HUS, TTP syndrome)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Two stages of baseline tests followed by more specific tests, after which each abnormal result should be weighed carefully:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Two Stages of Baseline Tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Baseline Set of Investigations: Blood count with a differential cell count Renal electrolytes and liver function tests C-reactive protein (CRP) and erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) Clotting screen Creatine kinase At least two blood cultures (while not receiving antibiotics) HIV test Chest X-ray Ultrasound of the abdomen Urine dipstick"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood count with a differential cell count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal electrolytes and liver function tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-reactive protein (CRP) and erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clotting screen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creatine kinase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At least two blood cultures (while not receiving antibiotics)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HIV test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound of the abdomen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urine dipstick"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Second-Line Investigations: Microbiological tests Imaging Biopsies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microbiological tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biopsies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood count with a differential cell count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal electrolytes and liver function tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-reactive protein (CRP) and erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clotting screen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creatine kinase"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At least two blood cultures (while not receiving antibiotics)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HIV test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound of the abdomen"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urine dipstick"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Microbiological tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Biopsies"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early blanket testing for all possible aetiologies is inappropriate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Each abnormal result should be weighed carefully."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray (Option A): Limited to evaluating respiratory causes and may miss abdominal pathologies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood cultures (Option B) are important for detecting infections but may not identify structural causes of the patient's symptoms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HIV test (Option D): Relevant for considering infectious etiologies but does not provide immediate insights into potential underlying structural abnormalities."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is not considered an autoinflammatory syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Familial Mediterranean fever"},{"t":"Adult-onset Still's disease"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"t":"Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Rheumatoid arthritis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rheumatoid arthritis is classified as an autoimmune disease, not an autoinflammatory syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Familial Mediterranean fever , Adult-onset Still’s disease , and Cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes , are recognized as autoinflammatory syndromes. (Option A, B, D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-infectious Inflammatory Disorder"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Category Examples Systemic Rheumatic and Autoimmune Diseases Ankylosing spondylitis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Behçet's disease Mixed connective-tissue disease Rheumatoid arthritis Sjögren's syndrome SLE Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome(VKH Syndrome)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vasculitis Allergic vasculitis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis Giant cell vasculitis/polymyalgia rheumatica Granulomatosis with polyangiitis Kawasaki disease Polyarteritis nodosa Takayasu arteritis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Granulomatous Diseases Idiopathic granulomatous hepatitis, sarcoidosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoinflammatory Syndromes (Option A, B, and Adult-onset Still's disease D ruled out) CAPS (cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Familial Mediterranean fever Hyper-IgD syndrome (HIDS, also known as mevalonate kinase deficiency) PAPA syndrome (pyogenic sterile arthritis, pyoderma gangrenosum, and acne) PFAPA syndrome (periodic fever, aphthous stomatitis, pharyngitis, adenitis) SAPHO (synovitis, acne, pustulosis, hyperostosis, osteomyelitis) TRAPS (tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated periodic syndrome)"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 30-year-old female presents with a fever of 39°C that has persisted for the past five days. She reports severe muscle pain, nausea, and retroorbital pain. On examination, she has mild dehydration and a positive tourniquet test. Which of the following investigations would be most appropriate to confirm the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Complete blood count"},{"t":"NS-1 Antigen test"},{"t":"Blood culture"},{"t":"Liver function tests"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) NS-1 Antigen test"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is a case of Dengue"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The NS-1 antigen test is specific for dengue and can be positive as early as Day 1 of infection."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic criteria:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fever: Must be present."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Any 2 of the following 6 features:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retroorbital pain, myalgia, or \"break-bone fever\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Protracted nausea or vomiting: Can lead to dehydration"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rash: Observed during the illness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Warning signs: Any 1 positive sign (e.g., severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Positive tourniquet test: >20 petechiae per square inch BP cuff inflated between systolic and diastolic blood pressure The cuff inflated for 5 minutes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">20 petechiae per square inch"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BP cuff inflated between systolic and diastolic blood pressure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cuff inflated for 5 minutes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leucopenia: Decreased white blood cell count"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">20 petechiae per square inch"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BP cuff inflated between systolic and diastolic blood pressure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cuff inflated for 5 minutes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Type Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ELISA IgM dengue virus (MAC-ELISA)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Positive by Day 4 of infection Indicates recent infection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"NAAT Detects viral RNA"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Useful for early diagnosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"NS-1 Antigen NS-1 (IgG)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Positive on Day 1 of infection Should be performed within 7 days of symptom onset"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete blood count (Option A): While helpful in assessing leukopenia and thrombocytopenia, it does not confirm dengue."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blood culture (Option C): This is more relevant for bacterial infections and is not specific for viral infections like dengue."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray (Option D): Not relevant in this context as it doesn't help diagnose dengue."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 38-year-old male presents with a one-week history of fever, headache, retro-orbital pain, and myalgia. Skin examination reveals multiple purpura and petechiae on the left shoulder, trunk, and legs. Similar cases have been reported linked to increased incidence of mosquito breeding. What is the most common neurological complication associated with this condition?","o":[{"t":"Encephalopathy"},{"t":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome"},{"t":"Stroke"},{"t":"Encephalitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Encephalopathy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation including fever, headache, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, and the presence of purpura and petechiae, suggests a viral infection consistent with dengue fever. The association with increased mosquito breeding further supports this diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Encephalopathy is the most common neurological complication associated with this condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dengue complications"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Neurological Dengue encephalopathy is most commonly encountered"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"neurological complication of dengue virus (Option A) linked primarily to dengue hemorrhagic fever and dengue shock syndrome EEG may show abnormalities such as burst suppression and seizures CSF profiles typically remain normal. Neuroimaging can be normal or reveal diffuse cerebral edema. Encephalitis (Option D ruled out) GBS (Option B ruled out) Stroke (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cardiomyopathy Dengue virus-induced dilated cardiomyopathy should"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"always be considered if a dengue fever patient has refractory shock and signs and symptoms of congestive heart failure."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hepatic injury Liver involvement in dengue is a crucial feature"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The effect ranges from an asymptomatic rise in liver enzymes to the development of ALF The most common atypical manifestation reported was hepatitis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pneumonia Staphylococcus pneumonia is an important"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"accompanying problem seen in dengue patient co-infection between dengue and influenza can result in exacerbation of pneumonia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Orchitis Orchitis usually occurs together with epididymitis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Commonly seen in men between 14 and 35 years of age."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dengue Hemorrhagic All of the following: Fever Acute onset of fever"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"2-7 days duration Hemorrhagic manifestations Positive tourniquet test/Petechiae Ecchymosis Purpura/Bleeding from mucosa, GIT, Injection sites, etc Platelet count £ 100,000 cells/cu.mm. Plasma leakage Rising hematocrit Hemoconcentration >20% Pleural effusion, ascites/Hypoproteinemia)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dengue Shock DHF criteria plus Signs of Shock Syndrome Tachycardia/Cool extremities/Weak"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"pulse/Lethargy/Restlessness Pulse pressure <20 mm Hg with increased Diastolic pressure (e.g. 100/80) Hypotension (Systolic <80 mm Hg for age <5 years/80-90 mm Hg for older child and adults)"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 28-year-old female presents with a fever of 39°C for the past three days. She reports muscle aches and mild retro-orbital pain but denies any gastrointestinal symptoms. On examination, she is alert, has a dry mouth, and a rash on her trunk, and her vital signs are stable. She has passed urine twice in the last six hours. Which of the following treatments is most appropriate for her management?","o":[{"t":"Start intravenous fluids"},{"t":"Administer ORS and paracetamol"},{"t":"Prescribe aspirin for fever"},{"t":"Admit to the hospital for observation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Administer ORS and paracetamol"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This case is suggestive of dengue fever, given the presentation of high fever, muscle aches, retro-orbital pain, and rash."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Given that the patient has no warning signs, is stable, and can tolerate oral fluids, she falls into the outpatient management category."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The appropriate treatment includes: ORS (Oral Rehydration Solution) to address mild dehydration. Paracetamol for fever control, while avoiding aspirin due to its bleeding risk in potential dengue cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ORS (Oral Rehydration Solution) to address mild dehydration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paracetamol for fever control, while avoiding aspirin due to its bleeding risk in potential dengue cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ORS (Oral Rehydration Solution) to address mild dehydration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paracetamol for fever control, while avoiding aspirin due to its bleeding risk in potential dengue cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Group A (Outpatient) No warning signs: Abdominal pain or tenderness Persistent vomiting Clinical fluid accumulation Mucosal bleed Lethargy or restlessness Liver enlargement > 2 cm Hematocrit count Rise >20% admission value Rapid decrease in platelet count And who are able to: Tolerate adequate volumes of oral fluids To pass urine at least once every 6 hours ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Group B (Inpatient) Warning signs present or Coexisting: Pregnancy, infancy, old age Obesity, diabetes mellitus, renal failure Chronic hemolytic diseases or Social: Living alone or living far from a health facility without reliable means of transport Dengue GROUP (B)\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"8501\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"625\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/1qshjXRP86UcXAEdMBkg1744285658. png\" width=\"600\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note: Group C (ICU) For Hemodynamically unstable Dengue"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IV fluids and Hospital admission (Options A and D) are not necessary given her stable condition and the absence of severe symptoms."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 35-year-old male presents to the hospital with fever, muscle aches, vomiting, and respiratory distress after consuming contaminated palm sap in a region known for bat activity. Laboratory tests reveal elevated white blood cell counts in his cerebrospinal fluid. Based on the clinical presentation and diagnostic findings, which of the following statements regarding Nipah virus infection is correct?","o":[{"t":"The virus is primarily transmitted from human to human through respiratory droplets."},{"t":"Supportive care is the mainstay of treatment, as there are no approved antiviral therapies specifically for Nipah virus."},{"t":"The incubation period for Nipah virus infection is usually less than 4 days."},{"t":"Ribavirin is the first-line antiviral treatment with established efficacy in humans."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Supportive care is the mainstay of treatment, as there are no approved antiviral therapies specifically for Nipah virus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nipah virus infection currently lacks approved antiviral treatments, making supportive care crucial for managing symptoms and complications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This includes maintaining vital functions, hydration, and monitoring."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While some agents like Ribavirin have been used, their effectiveness is not well-established, reinforcing the importance of supportive management in treating affected patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nipah Virus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Features"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transmission Primarily from bats (natural reservoir) to humans (Option A ruled out) Intermediate hosts: pigs Contaminated food products (e.g., palm sap) Human-to-human transmission through infected bodily fluids"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily from bats (natural reservoir) to humans (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intermediate hosts: pigs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contaminated food products (e.g., palm sap)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Human-to-human transmission through infected bodily fluids"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Presentation Incubation: 4 to 45 days (Option C ruled out) Symptoms: Fever Muscle aches Vomiting Respiratory distress Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incubation: 4 to 45 days (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Fever Muscle aches Vomiting Respiratory distress Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle aches"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory distress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology Infects respiratory epithelium Can cause acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) May affect multiple organs, leading to multi-organ failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infects respiratory epithelium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can cause acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May affect multiple organs, leading to multi-organ failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily from bats (natural reservoir) to humans (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intermediate hosts: pigs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contaminated food products (e.g., palm sap)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Human-to-human transmission through infected bodily fluids"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incubation: 4 to 45 days (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms: Fever Muscle aches Vomiting Respiratory distress Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle aches"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory distress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muscle aches"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vomiting"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory distress"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurological symptoms (e.g., seizures, altered consciousness, encephalitis)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infects respiratory epithelium"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Can cause acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"May affect multiple organs, leading to multi-organ failure"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Workup:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Diagnostic Method Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Laboratory Tests PCR for viral RNA detection"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ELISA for IgM and IgG antibodies CSF analysis for elevated WBC or protein levels"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Imaging Studies MRI or CT scans to assess neurological involvement"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(not specific for NiV)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment Options"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supportive Care Maintain airway patency Ensure fluid and electrolyte balance Monitor vital signs Intensive care for severe cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Maintain airway patency"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ensure fluid and electrolyte balance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitor vital signs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intensive care for severe cases"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antiviral Agents"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Maintain airway patency"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ensure fluid and electrolyte balance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitor vital signs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intensive care for severe cases"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Agent Notes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ribavirin Mixed results; efficacy remains controversial (Option D"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"m102.4 Monoclonal antibody, promising in animal studies, used"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"compassionately in humans"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Favipiravir Under investigation, requires further validation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Remdesivir Under investigation, requires further validation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Preventive Measures Avoid exposure to bats and pigs Strict infection control during outbreaks"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Avoid exposure to bats and pigs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Strict infection control during outbreaks"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Avoid exposure to bats and pigs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Strict infection control during outbreaks"}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fever, conjunctivitis, and a maculopapular rash after returning from a trip to Central America. Her clinical presentation is suggestive of Zika virus infection. Which of the following statements about Zika virus (ZIKV) is true?","o":[{"t":"Transmitted only through infected bodily fluids."},{"t":"Incubation period is 1 to 3 days."},{"t":"Complications include microcephaly and Guillain–Barré syndrome."},{"t":"Reliable diagnosis via serology in the first week."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Complications include microcephaly and Guillain–Barré syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Zika virus (ZIKV) infection can lead to serious complications such as congenital microcephaly in newborns and Guillain–Barré syndrome in adults. \\"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The virus is primarily transmitted through mosquito bites and sexual contact, with an incubation period of 3–12 days, making option C the only correct statement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Zika Virus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transmission: (Option A ruled out) Spread by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus mosquitoes (daytime feeders). Sexual transmission is possible."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spread by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus mosquitoes (daytime feeders)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sexual transmission is possible."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spread by Aedes aegypti and Aedes albopictus mosquitoes (daytime feeders)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sexual transmission is possible."},{"k":"img","v":10523},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Epidemiology: Initially identified in Africa and Asia. It has become an epidemic in the Caribbean and Central/South America since 2015."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initially identified in Africa and Asia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has become an epidemic in the Caribbean and Central/South America since 2015."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initially identified in Africa and Asia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has become an epidemic in the Caribbean and Central/South America since 2015."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of ZIKA Infection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Incubation period 3–12 days (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptomatic presentation Fever, arthralgia, conjunctivitis, maculopapular rash"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Possible complications Guillain–Barré syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Congenital microcephaly"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other congenital issues Cerebral calcification"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Deafness Vision problems Joint contractures (arthrogryposis) Hydrops fetalis Growth retardation"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Infection target Neural progenitor cells"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Workup for ZIKV Infection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Test Type Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Routine blood tests Possible leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, or elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"transaminases (often normal)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"PCR test Detects ZIKV in blood (first week) or urine (up to 14"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"days)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Serology Retrospective diagnosis; cross-reactivity with other"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"flaviviruses (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Plaque-reduction neutralisation Detects virus-specific neutralising antibodies to testing differentiate primary infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of ZIKV Infection"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Approach: Supportive care (specifics not detailed)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supportive care (specifics not detailed)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Supportive care (specifics not detailed)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prevention of ZIKV Infection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prevention Method Recommendations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mosquito bite prevention Primary method to avoid infection"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sexual health precautions Infected individuals: safe sex for 6 months"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Delay pregnancy for 6 months"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Asymptomatic travelers Safe sex and avoid pregnancy for 2 months after"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"returning"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vaccination status No vaccine currently available"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 32-year-old male presents with progressive bilateral lower limb weakness over the past 10 days. On examination, he has flaccid paralysis and absent deep tendon reflexes in the affected limbs. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis reveals a normal white cell count with elevated protein levels. Nerve conduction studies confirm a demyelinating pattern. Based on the Brighton criteria, which level of diagnostic certainty for Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) does this patient fulfill?","o":[{"t":"Level 1"},{"t":"Level 2"},{"t":"Level 3"},{"t":"Level 4"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Level 1"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Level 1 criteria are fulfilled since:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There is bilateral flaccid weakness ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absent deep tendon reflexes are noted."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monophasic course with progressive symptoms within a defined time frame."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF findings are consistent with albuminocytological dissociation (elevated protein with normal white cell count)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nerve conduction study (NCS) is supportive of GBS."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Brighton Criteria"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Levels of Diagnostic Certainty The Brighton criteria classify GBS diagnosis into four levels based on clinical and laboratory findings:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Level Criteria"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Level 1 No alternative diagnosis for weakness"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diminished/absent deep tendon reflexes in weak limbs Monophasic course: onset to nadir within 12 hours to 28 days Bilateral flaccid limb weakness CSF cell count < 50 cells/microlitre Elevated CSF protein concentration Nerve conduction study (NCS) consistent with GBS subtype"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Level 2 All Level 1 criteria met, with possible variations (e.g.,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"present reflexes in some cases). (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Level 3 Most Level 1 criteria met but lacking one or more key"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"features. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Level 4 Minimal criteria were met; these may include atypical or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"incomplete presentations. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sensitivity of the Brighton Criteria"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Level 1 : ~72%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Level 2 : ~96%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Level 3 : ~98%"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features aligning with the Brighton criteria include :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symmetrical limb weakness : Bilateral weakness is common."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diminished reflexes : Often absent or reduced in affected limbs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monophasic course : Rapid progression, peaking within weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis : Typically shows albuminocytological dissociation (elevated protein with normal white cell count)."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 40-year-old HIV-positive patient presents with fever, dry cough, and progressive shortness of breath. His CD4+ count is 150 cells/mm³. Chest X-ray shows bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of infection in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Primary infection acquired from a contaminated water source"},{"t":"Airborne transmission from an infected individual"},{"t":"Ingestion of fungal spores from environmental reservoirs"},{"t":"Reactivation of a previously dormant infection acquired during childhood"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Airborne transmission from an infected individual"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient with HIV and a low CD4+ count (150 cells/mm³), fever, dry cough, and bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest X-ray likely has Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP) . PJP is a common opportunistic infection in immunocompromised individuals and is primarily transmitted via airborne spread from person to person. The organism is inhaled, leading to infection in the lungs, especially when immune defenses are weakened."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pneumocystis jirovecii: Cause and Transmission"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Opportunistic Pathogen : Primarily affects immunocompromised individuals (e.g., HIV/AIDS patients with CD4+ counts <200 cells/mm³)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily affects immunocompromised individuals (e.g., HIV/AIDS patients with CD4+ counts <200 cells/mm³)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Colonization in Healthy Individuals : Common in healthy lungs; most children are exposed by age 4. Reactivation in adults is possible from childhood exposure or new infections."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common in healthy lungs; most children are exposed by age 4."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reactivation in adults is possible from childhood exposure or new infections."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Airborne Transmission : Spreads through person-to-person contact, especially in hospital settings. Colonized individuals exhale the organism, contaminating the environment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spreads through person-to-person contact, especially in hospital settings."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Colonized individuals exhale the organism, contaminating the environment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reinfection vs. Reactivation : Both reinfection with new strains and reactivation of dormant infection are possible causes of recurrent PCP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both reinfection with new strains and reactivation of dormant infection are possible causes of recurrent PCP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Environmental Reservoir : Exact environmental source unknown, but the organism has been found in air samples near infected patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exact environmental source unknown, but the organism has been found in air samples near infected patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primarily affects immunocompromised individuals (e.g., HIV/AIDS patients with CD4+ counts <200 cells/mm³)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common in healthy lungs; most children are exposed by age 4."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reactivation in adults is possible from childhood exposure or new infections."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spreads through person-to-person contact, especially in hospital settings."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Colonized individuals exhale the organism, contaminating the environment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both reinfection with new strains and reactivation of dormant infection are possible causes of recurrent PCP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exact environmental source unknown, but the organism has been found in air samples near infected patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Primary infection acquired from a contaminated water source (Option A): Pneumocystis jirovecii is not transmitted through water sources."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ingestion of fungal spores from environmental reservoirs (Option C) : There is no evidence suggesting transmission through ingestion of spores."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Reactivation of a previously dormant infection acquired during childhood (Option D) : Partially correct but not the best answer. Although reactivation can occur, the question hints at new infection rather than reactivation."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 45-year-old male with HIV presents with low-grade fever, nonproductive cough, and progressive shortness of breath over the past month. His HRCT shows bilateral ground-glass opacities. Which of the following statements correctly differentiates PCP in HIV-infected patients from non-HIV individuals?","o":[{"t":"PCP in HIV-infected patients usually present with acute onset and severe oxygenation impairment."},{"t":"Non-HIV patients with PCP experience a gradual onset with mild respiratory distress."},{"t":"HIV-infected individuals typically have a chronic presentation with milder respiratory distress initially."},{"t":"Mortality in HIV-infected PCP cases is higher (30-60%) compared to non-HIV individuals."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) HIV-infected individuals typically have a chronic presentation with milder respiratory distress initially."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In HIV-infected patients, PCP develops gradually over weeks to months with initially milder respiratory distress due to partial immune function, allowing a slower progression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In contrast, non-HIV patients experience acute onset and rapid respiratory decline, often leading to severe hypoxemia and higher mortality."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pneumocystis Jirovecii Pneumonia (PCP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCP is a major opportunistic infection in immunocompromised individuals, with varying symptoms based on immune status (HIV vs. non-HIV)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of PCP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"General Symptoms Fever Nonproductive cough Dyspnea (shortness of breath) Tachypnea (rapid breathing)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonproductive cough"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnea (shortness of breath)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachypnea (rapid breathing)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fever"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonproductive cough"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnea (shortness of breath)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachypnea (rapid breathing)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptom Variations by Immune Status Parameter HIV-Infected Patients Non-HIV Patients"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Onset Gradual (2 weeks to 2 months) Sudden (within a week) (Option B"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option A ruled out) ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptom Severi Milder respiratory distress initially Rapid progression to respiratory ty failure (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Course Chronic Acute"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Oxygen Impairm Less severe (Option A ruled out) More severe ent"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Physical examination findings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal lung sounds despite hypoxemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Signs of respiratory distress : Nasal flaring and retractions (in infants or severely affected adults)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nasal flaring and retractions (in infants or severely affected adults)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nasal flaring and retractions (in infants or severely affected adults)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Radiological findings"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HRCT : Bilateral interstitial pneumonia Ground-glass opacities and patchy consolidations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral interstitial pneumonia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ground-glass opacities and patchy consolidations"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bilateral interstitial pneumonia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ground-glass opacities and patchy consolidations"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prognosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Group Mortality Rate"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Non-HIV Patients 30%–60% (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"HIV-Infected Patients 10%–20%"}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 35-year-old immunocompromised patient presents with a persistent cough, dyspnea, and fever. A chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates. Sputum examination is inconclusive, and a bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) is performed. Which of the following diagnostic methods is most likely to provide the highest sensitivity for confirming the presence of Pneumocystis jirovecii?","o":[{"t":"Induced sputum microscopy"},{"t":"Chest X-ray"},{"t":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)"},{"t":"b-D-glucan assay"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCR is a highly sensitive method for detecting Pneumocystis jirovecii DNA, with sensitivity rates up to 96%."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is preferred over other methods, such as induced sputum microscopy or chest X-rays, which have lower sensitivity and may yield inconclusive results."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This high accuracy is crucial for diagnosing PCP, especially in immunocompromised patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Investigations for Pneumocystis Jirovecii Pneumonia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Assessment (History and Symptoms ):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focus on respiratory symptoms: cough, dyspnea, fever."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Particularly important for immunocompromised patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Imaging Studies"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Type Findings"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chest X-ray Bilateral infiltrates, ground-glass opacities. (Option B"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CT Scan More sensitive; shows ground-glass opacities, cystic"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"lesions."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Laboratory Tests"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Induced Sputum : Collected post-saline inhalation; stained (e.g., Grocott methenamine silver stain) for microscopy. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Procedure : Saline washing of bronchi for fluid analysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Staining and Microscopy : Visualizes Pneumocystis jirovecii cysts in BAL fluid."},{"k":"img","v":10534},{"k":"img","v":10537},{"k":"img","v":10542},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCR : Highly sensitive; detects Pneumocystis jirovecii DNA. Sensitivity up to 96%, specificity around 98%. Real-time PCR preferred over nested PCR for efficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Real-time PCR preferred over nested PCR for efficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LAMP : Rapid detection method for Pneumocystis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Real-time PCR preferred over nested PCR for efficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"b-D-glucan Assay : Indicates fungal infections; helps differentiate between colonization and active infection. (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Challenges in Diagnosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Traditional staining methods show low sensitivity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of Pneumocystis jirovecii DNA may indicate colonization rather than active infection."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 35-year-old male patient with a history of HIV presents with worsening dyspnea and a dry cough. A chest X-ray reveals bilateral infiltrates, and a diagnosis of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is made. Which of the following is the first-line treatment for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Pentamidine"},{"t":"Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX)"},{"t":"Atovaquone"},{"t":"Clindamycin with Primaquine"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The first-line treatment for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) is Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) due to its high efficacy against both mild and severe cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alternatives like Pentamidine and Atovaquone are reserved for patients intolerant to TMP/SMX or with milder disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, TMP/SMX remains the preferred choice in clinical practice."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Pneumocystis Jiroveci"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-Line Treatment: Trimethoprim/Sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dosage : Intravenous (IV) : TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day Administered every 6-8 hours Oral : Switch to oral TMP/SMX after clinical improvement at similar dosages."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous (IV) : TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day Administered every 6-8 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administered every 6-8 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral : Switch to oral TMP/SMX after clinical improvement at similar dosages."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duration : 21 days"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Efficacy : First-line treatment for both mild and severe PCP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous (IV) : TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day Administered every 6-8 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administered every 6-8 hours"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral : Switch to oral TMP/SMX after clinical improvement at similar dosages."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TMP: 15-20 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SMX: 75-100 mg/kg/day"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administered every 6-8 hours"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alternative Treatments"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Administration Indication"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pentamidine IV: 4 mg/kg/day For patients intolerant to TMP/SMX"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atovaquone Oral: 750 mg twice daily Effective for mild to moderate PCP,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"fewer side effects (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clindamycin + Combination therapy For patients failing TMP/SMX or Primaquine pentamidine (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dapsone Alone or with TMP For prophylaxis and treatment in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"select cases"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Caspofungin Combination with low-dose For severe cases"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"TMP/SMX"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Adjunctive Therapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corticosteroids : Recommended for moderate to severe PCP, especially with low oxygen levels (PaO2 < 70 mm Hg or oxygen saturation < 92%) . Regimen : Prednisone, starting with high doses, tapering over days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regimen : Prednisone, starting with high doses, tapering over days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regimen : Prednisone, starting with high doses, tapering over days."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"10497":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10534":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10503":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10537":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10508":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10542":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10523":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","10492":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});